## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKvNV5wXQAAHAuV?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/neil%5Fxbt/article/2065971076673253462/media/2065970630244122624) How to Build a Personal Knowledge Agent With Hermes and Claude That Connects Everything You Learn. [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2060131227756072962/s8DV8v8L_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/neil%5Fxbt)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>NeilXbt</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/neil_xbt">@neil_xbt</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065923035916361728">http://x.com/i/article/2065923035916361728</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065923035916361728">http://x.com/i/article/2065923035916361728</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKvNV5wXQAAHAuV?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/neil%5Fxbt/article/2065971076673253462/media/2065970630244122624)<br/>How to Build a Personal Knowledge Agent With Hermes and Claude That Connects Everything You Learn.<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2060131227756072962/s8DV8v8L_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/neil%5Fxbt)<br/>[NeilXbt](http://x.com/neil%5Fxbt)<br/>[@neil\_xbt](http://x.com/neil%5Fxbt)<br/>·<br/>5h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow neil\_xbt<br/>13<br/>14<br/>67<br/>[5.3K](http://x.com/neil%5Fxbt/status/2065971076673253462/analytics)<br/>Everything you learn is disappearing.<br/>The article you read last Tuesday that changed how you think about your work. <br/>The insight from a podcast three weeks ago that connected two ideas you had been holding separately. The solution to a problem you solved in a conversation last month and will have to solve again because you do not remember how you got there. The book you finished that felt important while you were reading it and is now a vague impression of having read something good.<br/>This is not a memory failure. It is an architectural failure. <br/>Human memory was never designed to retain the volume of information a modern knowledge worker processes, and no amount of discipline fixes the underlying problem. The notes app you abandoned, the highlighting you never review, the bookmarks folder with four hundred links you will never open again, these are all attempts to solve an architecture problem with willpower. Willpower loses.<br/>The reason previous attempts failed is that they all required you to be the connection-maker. You read something, and then you had to decide where it filed, what it related to, and how to find it later. That decision, made hundreds of times a week at the exact moment you are busy with something else, is the point where every personal knowledge system collapses.<br/>A personal knowledge agent removes the human from the connection-making. You feed it what you learn. It stores everything permanently, searches across everything you have ever told it, finds the connections between new information and everything already there, and surfaces relevant knowledge when you need it without you remembering it exists.<br/>The combination that makes this possible in 2026 is Hermes Agent for the persistent memory and Claude for the reasoning. Hermes remembers everything across every session, indefinitely. Claude builds the connections and produces the synthesis. Together, they create a knowledge system that compounds across months instead of evaporating after each conversation.<br/>This article is the complete build.<br/>## <br/>Why This Was Impossible Until Recently<br/>The personal knowledge agent has been the obvious idea for years and impossible to build well for a specific technical reason: agents forgot everything between sessions.<br/>Every standard AI assistant operates with no memory across conversations. You explain something in one session, and the next session starts from zero. For a knowledge system, this is fatal. The entire value of a knowledge base is that it accumulates. An assistant that forgets everything cannot accumulate anything. It can answer a question about what you tell it right now, but it cannot connect what you tell it today to what you told it last month, because last month does not exist for it.<br/>Hermes Agent changed this by building memory in as the core architecture rather than bolting it on as a feature. Hermes runs a three-tier memory system that most agents do not have.<br/>Prompt memory loads your personal notes and preferences into the system prompt at the start of every session, so the agent begins each conversation already knowing the persistent facts about you and your work. Episodic memory is a full searchable archive of every past conversation, built so the agent can search across all previous sessions and recall anything yo</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 109 · 🔁 23 · 💬 13 · 🔖 95</p><p><a href="https://x.com/neil_xbt/status/2065971076673253462">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
