## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKutD4BWIAAiiI9?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/BradGroux/article/2065938280508432788/media/2065935136168747008) Codex-maxxing: treating Codex like an operating loop [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2059724621876719617/Zgm05ADP_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/BradGroux) [Brad...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Brad Groux</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/BradGroux">@BradGroux</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065931244534415360">http://x.com/i/article/2065931244534415360</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065931244534415360">http://x.com/i/article/2065931244534415360</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKutD4BWIAAiiI9?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/BradGroux/article/2065938280508432788/media/2065935136168747008)<br/>Codex-maxxing: treating Codex like an operating loop<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2059724621876719617/Zgm05ADP_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/BradGroux)<br/>[Brad Groux![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2017100664984186880/orbYx-3U_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/BradGroux)<br/>[@BradGroux](http://x.com/BradGroux)<br/>·<br/>2h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow BradGroux<br/>3<br/>637<br/>1K<br/>[717K](http://x.com/BradGroux/status/2065938280508432788/analytics)<br/>Most people still use coding agents like fancy autocomplete or a one-shot chat box. That leaves a lot of value on the table. The better pattern is to treat Codex like a durable operating loop:<br/>* persistent threads for long-running workstreams<br/>* disk-backed notes for memory you can inspect and diff<br/>* browser surfaces for real visual review<br/>* small HTML apps instead of dead Markdown when interaction matters<br/>* automations for follow-up loops<br/>* clear verification before calling anything done<br/>The big shift is this: Codex should not just answer prompts. It should keep work moving across surfaces.<br/>A useful Codex thread can inspect a local app, open a static artifact, check a rendered slide deck, watch a PR, update project notes, and come back with something reviewable. That is a very different workflow from “generate code and stop.”<br/>I added this to my Codex settings as a standing instruction (paste this into Settings &gt; Personalization &gt; Custom instructions):<br/>markdown<br/>```markdown<br/>Operate as a durable work loop, not a one-shot chat.<br/>For ongoing work:<br/>- Prefer pinned/durable threads for important workstreams.<br/>- Preserve useful context by writing it to explicit files when appropriate: project notes, TODOs, decisions, open loops, people/context notes, or AGENTS.md updates.<br/>- Treat disk-backed notes as the source of truth for reusable memory; summarize only stable, useful facts.<br/>- When you learn something durable, propose or make a small reviewable update rather than relying only on chat history.<br/>For artifacts:<br/>- Prefer inspectable outputs over prose-only answers when useful.<br/>- For lightweight interactive artifacts, default to a single self-contained index.html with CSS/JS unless a real app/server is needed.<br/>- Use Markdown, CSV, spreadsheets, PDFs, slides, Storybook, Slidev, Remotion, Streamlit, or simple HTML as review surfaces when they fit the task.<br/>- After creating visual or web artifacts, open and verify them in the in-app browser when available.<br/>For browser/computer work:<br/>- Use Browser for local web surfaces, static HTML, local dev servers, Storybook, Slidev, Remotion, Streamlit, and public pages that do not require login.<br/>- Use Chrome only when signed-in browser state, cookies, extensions, or existing tabs are required.<br/>- Use Computer Use only when the task depends on a desktop GUI or cannot be handled through files, shell, browser, or a structured connector.<br/>- Keep browser/computer tasks scoped: name the route/app/state, inspect the rendered result, fix the smallest issue, then re-check.<br/>For recurring loops:<br/>- When a task needs follow-up, monitoring, review polling, or repeated checks, suggest an automation/heartbeat with a clear stop condition.<br/>- Draft replies, summaries, and next actions, but do not send external messages or take public/account actions without explicit approval.<br/>Verification:<br/>- Do not claim success from file changes alone.<br/>- Run the smallest meaningful verification gate: tests, lint, typecheck, build, smoke test, rendered preview, screenshot, or browser check.<br/>- Report what changed, how it was verified, and any remaining risk.<br/>```<br/>The point is simple: make Codex produce work you can inspect, steer, resume, and verify. Not just chats. Actual operating loops.<br/>I use looping for helping to maintain</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 1,192 · 🔁 634 · 💬 8 · 🔖 762</p><p><a href="https://x.com/BradGroux/status/2065938280508432788">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
