## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKsS-SaW8AAGRGi?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/zostaff/article/2065765842709590018/media/2065765715383087104) Prompt Engineering: How It Actually Works [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1995248671483482112/dZ1-JoSj_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/zostaff) [zostaff](http://x.com/zostaff) [@zostaff](http://x.com/zostaff) · 4h Follow...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>zostaff</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/zostaff">@zostaff</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065547821441265664">http://x.com/i/article/2065547821441265664</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065547821441265664">http://x.com/i/article/2065547821441265664</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKsS-SaW8AAGRGi?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/zostaff/article/2065765842709590018/media/2065765715383087104)<br/>Prompt Engineering: How It Actually Works<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1995248671483482112/dZ1-JoSj_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/zostaff)<br/>[zostaff](http://x.com/zostaff)<br/>[@zostaff](http://x.com/zostaff)<br/>·<br/>4h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow zostaff<br/>1<br/>2<br/>27<br/>[1.1K](http://x.com/zostaff/status/2065765842709590018/analytics)<br/>Most prompt guides are a list of tips at the level of "be specific" and "give context." They are not wrong, but they miss the main thing: why those tips work. And without the why, you cannot solve a new problem that was not on the list. You will copy other people's templates without understanding why one works and a similar one does not.<br/>This article is different. First we break down what happens inside the model when it reads your prompt. Then from that mechanics we derive the principles of thinking. And only then the rules, each of which will be a consequence of the mechanics, not an arbitrary "do it this way." Once you understand the machinery, the rules stop being incantations and become engineering decisions.<br/>## <br/>What a prompt actually does<br/>At every moment a language model predicts the next token from a probability distribution over the whole vocabulary. Without a prompt that distribution is smeared across everything the model saw in training. The request "write about a dog" leaves a gigantic space of possible continuations: an encyclopedia entry, a poem, a training manual, anything.<br/>A prompt is the narrowing of that probability space. Each refinement cuts away part of the distribution. "Write" narrows it to text. "Three paragraphs" narrows the length. "For a veterinarian" narrows the register. "No marketing cliches" cuts away a whole layer of phrasings. A good prompt is not an incantation, it is a sequence of constraints that compresses the space of possible answers down to the narrow region you need.<br/>From this follows the first principle of thinking, and it flips the beginner's approach. You are not persuading the model. You are narrowing its choice. The question is not "how do I ask more nicely" but "which constraints will cut away everything except what I need." Every sentence in a prompt should remove uncertainty, not add politeness.<br/>## <br/>How the model reads context: three facts from the architecture<br/>To write prompts deliberately, you need to know three things about how a transformer processes text. They are not theoretical, they directly dictate how to lay out a prompt.<br/>Fact one: long context works against focus, for two different reasons. The first is purely computational: the attention mechanism relates every token to every other token, which is O(n²), so doubling the length quadruples the work and hits latency and cost. The second matters more for quality: the more irrelevant text in the window, the more the model's attention smears across it, and the harder it is to see what is central. These are not the same thing. Quadratic cost is about price and speed, attention dilution is about accuracy. But the conclusion is shared: extra context is not free, neither in money nor in quality, and a long prompt is almost never better than a short and precise one.<br/>Fact two: the middle sinks, and the more so the longer the context. This is a documented effect, lost in the middle. The 2024 study by Liu and coauthors showed a U-shaped curve on long contexts: accuracy is highest when the needed information is at the beginning or the end, and drops by more than 30 percent if it is buried in the middle. An important caveat usually left out: the effect grows with length. On a prompt of a couple of paragraphs it can almost be ignored, on a long context with many documents it is critical. The practical takeaway scales with the</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 48 · 🔁 3 · 💬 4 · 🔖 91</p><p><a href="https://x.com/zostaff/status/2065765842709590018">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
