## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKn2ZqEbMAA8JHM?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow/article/2065454698711437810/media/2065452824776093696) Is the Giant Monolithic Model the Natural Endpoint? [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1806885734357549056/3JYRNGq5_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow) [deep Manifold](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow)...
- https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKwx2kTaoAAurrc.jpg: https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKwx2kTaoAAurrc.jpg</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>deep Manifold</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/BetaTomorrow">@BetaTomorrow</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065452779955748864">http://x.com/i/article/2065452779955748864</a></p></blockquote><div><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKwx2kTaoAAurrc.jpg" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065452779955748864">http://x.com/i/article/2065452779955748864</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKn2ZqEbMAA8JHM?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow/article/2065454698711437810/media/2065452824776093696)<br/>Is the Giant Monolithic Model the Natural Endpoint?<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1806885734357549056/3JYRNGq5_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow)<br/>[deep Manifold](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow)<br/>[@BetaTomorrow](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow)<br/>·<br/>20h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow BetaTomorrow<br/>2<br/>13<br/>[1K](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow/status/2065454698711437810/analytics)<br/>Chinese Edition: <br/>[巨型单体模型是自然终点吗?](https://x.com/BetaTomorrow/status/2065452701950046255)<br/>Recent frontier models with multi-trillion parameter counts raise an unavoidable question: is the giant monolithic model the natural endpoint of AI?<br/>The answer is not obvious. There are good theoretical reasons why AI has moved toward larger models, but there are also reasons to doubt that a single ever-larger model is the final architecture of intelligence.<br/>## <br/>Why So Many Parameters Are Theoretically Understandable<br/>Data complexity is rooted in high-order nonlinearity. The world is not low-order, smooth, or uniform, so a neural network needs sufficient degrees of freedom to represent it.<br/>This is evident in physical systems. A mountain range, a mountain, a rock joint, a rock sample, and a microscope image of rock may all belong to the same domain, yet each exhibits different nonlinear structure. The mountain range reflects large-scale geometry. The rock joint introduces discontinuity, contact, friction, and block interaction. The microscope image reveals granular structure, micro-cracks, and local discontinuities.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKn2ubIb0AA-vzQ?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/BetaTomorrow/article/2065454698711437810/media/2065453181543632896)<br/>From the Deep Manifold perspective, the central issue is data complexity. Data complexity is not only data volume. It also comes from scale, scope, time, discontinuity, and high-order nonlinearity. A model facing the real world must handle many scales at once, and each scale may contain its own nonlinear structure.<br/>This is one reason extremely large models are understandable. A trillion-parameter model is not simply a large memory. It is a huge numerical host for many local neural operators. Large parameter count provides the degrees of freedom needed to form many stacked piecewise manifolds, local covers, intrinsic pathways, and possible fixed-point behaviors.<br/>So the rise of giant models is not irrational. Data complexity demands freedom. High-order nonlinearity demands local flexibility. Multi-scale data demands many manifolds. But this does not mean the largest monolithic model is the final answer.<br/>[Single Token Geometry 03: Data Complexity](https://x.com/BetaTomorrow/status/2050461737363951997)<br/>## <br/>Geometry Challenge of the Giant Monolithic Model<br/>Trillions of parameters also expose the theoretical weakness of the giant monolithic model. The problem begins when a single model must carry too many scales, nonlinear regimes, and local geometries within one body. A giant model can host many manifolds, but hosting many manifolds is not the same as maintaining coherent structure across them. As data becomes more heterogeneous, the challenge is no longer merely representation capacity; it is preserving compatibility, continuity, and homology across regions that obey different mathematical demands.<br/>High-order nonlinear and discontinuous data makes this coherence problem much harder. Smooth regions may connect through gentle transitions, but contact, friction, local failure, sharp constraints, and path-dependent behavior require boundary-sensitive local structures. When all these regimes are forced into one monolithic body, different local geometries begin to compete: some regions prefer smooth interpolation, while other</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 14 · 🔁 2 · 🔖 7</p><p><a href="https://x.com/BetaTomorrow/status/2065454698711437810">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
      <media:content url="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKwx2kTaoAAurrc.jpg" medium="image" />
    </item>
