      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>AlphaSignal AI</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/AlphaSignalAI">@AlphaSignalAI</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065615613813288962">http://x.com/i/article/2065615613813288962</a></p></blockquote><div><img src="https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logo-fb.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://static.arxiv.org/icons/twitter/arxiv-logo-twitter-square.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/icons/cu/cornell-reduced-white-SMALL.svg" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logo-one-color-white.svg" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logomark-small-white.svg" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://arxiv.org/icons/licenses/by-sa-4.0.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/icons/social/bibsonomy.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/><img src="https://arxiv.org/static/browse/0.3.4/images/icons/social/reddit.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065615613813288962">http://x.com/i/article/2065615613813288962</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKqc46UXIAA3Tka?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065625151383732422/media/2065635880644059136)<br/>How SIA's Self-Improving Loop Works, And How to Actually Run It <br/>[![Square profile picture](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2014100845189529600/Ff1Xc28-_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>[AlphaSignal AI](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>[@AlphaSignalAI](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI)<br/>·<br/>3h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow AlphaSignalAI<br/>1<br/>1<br/>5<br/>[483](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/status/2065625151383732422/analytics)<br/>A Feedback-Agent that rewrites scaffolds and trains LoRA weights, three SOTA results, an MIT repo, and a 10-minute setup<br/>&gt; In \~8 mins: the 3-agent loop, the harness-vs-weights ablation across 3 benchmarks, a 6-step install-to-run tutorial, the verifier contract for your own tasks, and the one place the public repo diverges from the paper.<br/>SIA just hit 70.1% Top-1 accuracy on LawBench's 191-class Chinese legal benchmark, where the prior best was 45.0%.<br/>Hexo Labs released it on May 28 as an MIT-licensed repo, alongside an arXiv paper titled "SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness &amp; Weight Updates" co-authored with the University of Oxford.<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1763090471822536704/l57OOjTj_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/kunalbhatia91)<br/>Kunal Bhatia<br/>@kunalbhatia91<br/>·<br/>[May 28](http://x.com/kunalbhatia91/status/2060013228231725092)<br/>Superintelligence will be built on Self Improvement. Today <br/>[@hexoai](http://x.com/hexoai)<br/>, we’re excited to release ‘SIA’ - an open-source Self-Improving AI, to achieve any goal through recursive self improvement. While trying to solve a problem, SIA doesn't just improve it's abilities by updating<br/>Show more<br/>![](https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2060003043698225152/img/HHSLcFI59c-w9fbq.jpg)<br/>121<br/>226<br/>638<br/>[516K](http://x.com/kunalbhatia91/status/2060013228231725092/analytics)<br/>The pitch: one loop where an AI rewrites the agent's scaffold and retrains the model's weights, two levers prior research only pulled separately.<br/>The catch: the paper's version picks the lever automatically. The version you can run today asks you to pick it yourself.<br/># <br/>What SIA actually is<br/>Every agent is two things glued together. The harness (system prompt, tools, parsers, retry logic) decides how the model works the problem, and the weights decide what the model knows.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKqSNLJWcAM4myD?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065625151383732422/media/2065624134130757635)<br/>Self-improvement research split along that seam. One school has a meta-agent rewrite the scaffold while the weights stay frozen, the other trains weights at test time while the scaffold stays frozen.<br/>SIA runs both levers in a single loop. When scaffold edits stop paying, it trains the model instead, using the task's own scoring function as the reward.<br/># <br/>Three benchmarks, one clean ablation<br/>The paper isolates each lever's contribution. SIA-H is harness updates only, SIA-W+H adds weight updates on top.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKqM-YIXMAAfGnt?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/AlphaSignalAI/article/2065625151383732422/media/2065618382360096768)<br/>The TriMul row translates to a 1,017 µs kernel against the prior SOTA's 1,161 µs, a 14.02x speedup over SIA's own baseline. Adding weight updates after the harness phase cut 91.9% off the harness-only peak of 12,483 µs.<br/>The most quotable result is two lines of NumPy. On the denoising task, the first weight-update checkpoint added an np.clip \+ np.rint step that rounds imputed gene counts to non-negative integers, a biological constraint no scaffold edit produced across every harness iteration.<br/>One caveat before you screenshot the table. All numbers come from the authors' own verifier setup (paper, Table 3), with no</blockquote><p>🔗 <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27276">[2605.27276] SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness &amp; Weight Updates</a></p><blockquote>Skip to main content Learn about arXiv becoming an independent nonprofit. We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, member institutions , and all contributors. Donate &gt; cs &gt; arXiv:2605.27276 Help | Advanced Search All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text Search GO quick links Login Help Pages About Computer Science &gt; Artificial Intelligence arXiv:2605.27276 (cs) [Submitted on 26 May 2026 ( v1 ), last revised 28 May 2026 (this version, v2)] Title: SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness &amp; Weight Updates Authors: Prannay Hebbar , Yogendra Manawat , Samuel Verboomen , Alesia Ivanova , Selvam Palanimalai , Kunal Bhatia , Vignesh Baskaran View a PDF of the paper titled SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness &amp; Weight Updates, by Prannay Hebbar and 6 other authors View PDF HTML (experimental) Abstract: Humans are the bottleneck in building and improving AI. Both the models and the agents that wrap them are written, tuned, and corrected by people. The long-horizon goal of an AI that can figure out how to improve itself remains open. Two largely disjoint research lines attack this bottleneck. The harness-update school has a meta-agent rewrite the scaffold of a task-specific agent (its tools, prompts, retry logic, and search procedure) while the model weights are held fixed. The test-time training school uses hand-written RL pipelines to update the model's own weights on task feedback while the harness is held fixed. These two silos operate in isolation. We propose SIA, a self-improving loop in which a language-model agent (the Feedback-Agent) updates both the harness and the weights of a task-specific agent. We evaluate across three contrasting domains: Chinese legal charge classification, low-level GPU kernel optimisation, and single-cell RNA denoising. Combining both levers outperforms scaffold iteration alone on all three benchmarks. SIA-W+H achieves 25.1% over prior SOTA on LawBench, 12.4% faster GPU kernels than prior SOTA (1,017 vs 1,161 {\mu}s), and 20.4% over prior SOTA on denoising. Harness updates make the model agentic, shaping how it searches and acts, while weight updates build the domain intuition that no prompt or scaffold can instil. Subjects: Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI) ; Computation and Language (cs.CL) ACM classes: I.2.6; I.2.11; I.2.8 Cite as: arXiv:2605.27276 [cs.AI] (or arXiv:2605.27276v2 [cs.AI] for this version) https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2605.27276 Focus to learn more arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite Submission history From: Prannay Hebbar [ view email ] [v1] Tue, 26 May 2026 16:55:46 UTC (221 KB) [v2] Thu, 28 May 2026 08:21:29 UTC (302 KB) Full-text links: Access Paper: View a PDF of the paper titled SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness &amp; Weight Updates, by Prannay Hebbar and 6 other authors View PDF HTML (experimental) TeX Source view license Current browse context: cs.AI &lt; prev | next &gt; new | recent | 2026-05 Change to browse by: cs cs.CL References &amp; Citations NASA ADS Google Scholar Semantic Scholar export BibTeX citation Loading... BibTeX formatted citation × loading... Data provided by: Bookmark Bibliographic Tools Bibliographic and Citation Tools Bibliographic Explorer Toggle Bibliographic Explorer ( What is the Explorer? ) Connected Papers Toggle Connected Papers ( What is Connected Papers? ) Litmaps Toggle Litmaps ( What is Litmaps? ) scite.ai Toggle scite Smart Citations ( What are Smart Citations? ) Code, Data, Media Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article alphaXiv Toggle alphaXiv ( What is alphaXiv? ) Links to Code Toggle CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers ( What is CatalyzeX? ) DagsHub Toggle DagsHub ( What is DagsHub? ) GotitPub Toggle Gotit.pub ( What is GotitPub? ) Huggingface Toggle Hugging Face ( What is Huggingface? ) ScienceCast Toggle ScienceCast ( What is ScienceCast? ) Demos Demos Replicate Toggle Replicate ( What is Replicate? )</blockquote><p>🔗 <a href="https://github.com/hexo-ai/sia">GitHub - hexo-ai/sia: SIA is a Self Improving AI framework to autonomously improve the performance of any AI system (Model / Agent) on a benchmark task. · GitHub</a></p><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 5