## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKoegrEbkAAsb-d?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ/article/2065496975097933910/media/2065496925768749056) how to go from data to market intelligence in 30 mins [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2061733958577188864/_mf-k06e_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ) [Vibe Marketers HQ](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Vibe Marketers HQ</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/vibemarketersHQ">@vibemarketersHQ</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065495068111482880">http://x.com/i/article/2065495068111482880</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065495068111482880">http://x.com/i/article/2065495068111482880</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKoegrEbkAAsb-d?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ/article/2065496975097933910/media/2065496925768749056)<br/>how to go from data to market intelligence in 30 mins<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2061733958577188864/_mf-k06e_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ)<br/>[Vibe Marketers HQ](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ)<br/>[@vibemarketersHQ](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ)<br/>·<br/>15h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow vibemarketersHQ<br/>2<br/>37<br/>[1.3K](http://x.com/vibemarketersHQ/status/2065496975097933910/analytics)<br/>having access to data does not mean you have intelligence.<br/>[nassim taleb](https://fs.blog/noise-and-signal-nassim-taleb/)<br/>,  the guy who literally wrote the book on probability said data is toxic in large quantities.<br/>&gt; more data without a framework to interpret it = more noise you mistake for signal. More certainty about the wrong things.<br/>[86% of marketers](https://martech.org/marketers-are-drowning-in-data-but-starving-for-insight/)<br/>can't tell you what's actually driving their performance.<br/>not because they have too little data. they just never built the layer that converts data into something you can act on.<br/>the cia calls it <br/>[finished intelligence](https://irp.fas.org/cia/product/facttell/intcycle.htm)<br/>[russell ackoff](https://faculty.ung.edu/kmelton/documents/datawisdom.pdf)<br/>, who built the academic model for this in 1989, called it the move from information to knowledge to understanding.the cia calls it <br/>intelligence isn't collected. It's built through a process by someone who knows what to ask and in what order.<br/>most people skip that process. they go from data straight to content and then wonder why nothing lands.<br/>## <br/>here is what I mean:<br/>```text<br/>data + research = gap<br/>gap + angle = intelligence<br/>intelligence → content strategy, product, <br/>positioning whatever you're building<br/>```<br/>| aspect       | what it means                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | example                                                                                                       |<br/>| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |<br/>| data         | what exists. trend reports, search volumes, competitor posts, audience comments, platform analytics. raw. unfiltered. available to everyone in your market.                                                                          | "short-form video is growing. founders are posting on linkedin. ai tools are trending."                       |<br/>| research     | the process of asking the right questions of that data in the right order. not one search. not five open tabs. a deliberate sequence that starts broad and narrows until the market shows you something it hasn't shown anyone else. | "who in this space is NOT being served by the content that exists? what are they asking that nobody answers?" |<br/>| gap          | what the market needs that nobody is giving it yet. not an assumption. not a hunch. what the research surfaces when you look for absence instead of presence.                                                                        | "founders building with ai have no resource that teaches market intelligence, only content production."       |<br/>| angle        | your specific way of owning that gap. the gap belongs to the market. the angle belongs to you. the intersection of the gap and what you can credibly own.</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 50 · 🔁 3 · 🔖 81</p><p><a href="https://x.com/vibemarketersHQ/status/2065496975097933910">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>@ericosiu: DO NOT FALL BEHIND Fable 5 is about to pick a winner in your niche. Make sure it's you....</title>
      <link>https://x.com/ericosiu/status/2065456339183390743</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">2065456339183390743</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 12 Jun 2026 15:29:00 GMT</pubDate>
      <dc:creator>ericosiu</dc:creator>
      <description>DO NOT FALL BEHIND
&gt;
&gt; Fable 5 is about to pick a winner in your niche.
&gt;
&gt; Make sure it's you.
&gt;
&gt; A new frontier model is a rare window. For a short stretch, a one-person shop can outrun a company ten times its size. Then the window closes and the new capability becomes table stakes.
&gt;
&gt; In six months this will be normal. Your whole niche will be running it. The advantage goes to whoever started today and spent those months compounding while the rest caught up.
&gt;
&gt; So the only question that matters is this.
&gt;
&gt; When your competitor finally figures this out, are they catching up to you? Or are you catching up to them?
&gt;
&gt; Pick one problem in your business that costs you money.
&gt;
&gt; Point Fable 5 at it this week.
&gt;
