## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKgAirPWsAAPWVd?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/elvissun/article/2065035615800864954/media/2064901024872050688) /goal + Loss Functions: How to Distill a Product in 30 Hours with One Prompt \[Full Playbook\] [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1886389973236011008/7EZHFw9k_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/elvissun)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Elvis</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/elvissun">@elvissun</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2058192123556405248">http://x.com/i/article/2058192123556405248</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2058192123556405248">http://x.com/i/article/2058192123556405248</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKgAirPWsAAPWVd?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/elvissun/article/2065035615800864954/media/2064901024872050688)<br/>/goal + Loss Functions: How to Distill a Product in 30 Hours with One Prompt \[Full Playbook\]<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1886389973236011008/7EZHFw9k_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/elvissun)<br/>[Elvis](http://x.com/elvissun)<br/>[@elvissun](http://x.com/elvissun)<br/>·<br/>14h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow elvissun<br/>9<br/>45<br/>435<br/>[35K](http://x.com/elvissun/status/2065035615800864954/analytics)<br/>99% people are using /goal and loops wrong.<br/>The hype they hear is "long-running loops prompting autonomous agent": point it at a task, walk away, come back to working code.<br/>But top agentic engineers have been doing that without /goal for 6 months (when GPT-5.2 and Opus 4.5 came out). It's called harness engineering + spec-driven development: <br/>1. build a harness for agent to observe the problem<br/>2. write a tight spec with all the test cases<br/>3. let Codex or Claude Code loop unattended until it meets every one.<br/>I kick these off overnight constantly — 2-5 hours a run. In April one chewed through a Turbo build-cache bug across our Vercel monorepo and had it green by morning. No /goal is actually required. <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1886389973236011008/7EZHFw9k_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/elvissun)<br/>Elvis<br/>@elvissun<br/>·<br/>[Apr 10](http://x.com/elvissun/status/2042633997080224034)<br/>i'll say this again because i keep seeing people do it wrong: you can solve ANY engineering problem by dropping an agent with the right harness in a loop. codex just one-shotted our turbo cache fix after I gave it everything it needs to debug like a real dev on the team. would<br/>Show more<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HFjky8zakAAz0LL?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/elvissun/status/2042633997080224034/photo/1)<br/>Quote<br/>![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1886389973236011008/7EZHFw9k_mini.jpg)<br/>Elvis<br/>@elvissun<br/>·<br/>Mar 26<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HEVQ8m7bYAA4jkW?format=jpg&amp;name=240x240)](http://x.com/elvissun/status/2037123410736910341/photo/1)<br/>software has changed forever: you can solve literally ANY engineering problem if you can: - stop trying to solve the problem yourself - build a harness for agents to take control of it - drop it in its own feedback loop until it's solved  x.com/elvissun/statu…<br/>32<br/>97<br/>1.2K<br/>[286K](http://x.com/elvissun/status/2042633997080224034/analytics)<br/>## <br/>So what's /goal actually for? <br/>Here's what one single prompt did while I was away:<br/>* \~30 hours, 6,300 lines of code, 92k pages crawled, $40 spent on APIs<br/>* Clone the core loop of another product — entire architecture reverse-engineered from scratch<br/>* Our version's output was \~50× better than the reference product on the same queries. (This is a new data layer that'll power  <br/>[newsjack.sh](https://newsjack.sh/)  <br/> \- the open-source news-intel skills I've been working on)<br/>The secret is loss function development (LFD): you write the agent a target to optimize toward, not a spec to build. <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1131851609774985216/OcsssQ9J_normal.png)](http://x.com/steipete)<br/>Peter Steinberger ![🦞](https://abs.twimg.com/emoji/v2/svg/1f99e.svg)<br/>![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2017100664984186880/orbYx-3U_bigger.jpg)<br/>@steipete<br/>·<br/>[Jun 7](http://x.com/steipete/status/2063697162748260627)<br/>Here’s your monthly reminder that you shouldn’t be prompting coding agents anymore. You should be designing loops that prompt your agents.<br/>1.7K<br/>2.7K<br/>19K<br/>[8.2M](http://x.com/steipete/status/2063697162748260627/analytics)<br/>This is a concrete example of Peter's tweet, operationalized.<br/>The spec used in spec-driven development now becomes the starting point, no longer the finish line.<br/>It took me some experimenting to get this right. But here's the entire p</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 813 · 🔁 73 · 💬 18 · 🔖 2,720</p><p><a href="https://x.com/elvissun/status/2065035615800864954">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
