## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKhy4DWaEAAwTVh?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/cgtwts/article/2065026197734797813/media/2065026736446902272) How a Solo Agency Owner Used Claude to Run Like a Five-Person Business [![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1955738644528160769/cgVyKTcs_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/cgtwts)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>CG</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/cgtwts">@cgtwts</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065020060570370048">http://x.com/i/article/2065020060570370048</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2065020060570370048">http://x.com/i/article/2065020060570370048</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKhy4DWaEAAwTVh?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/cgtwts/article/2065026197734797813/media/2065026736446902272)<br/>How a Solo Agency Owner Used Claude to Run Like a Five-Person Business<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1955738644528160769/cgVyKTcs_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/cgtwts)<br/>[CG![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1993778330659901440/xZ32FAxg_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/cgtwts)<br/>[@cgtwts](http://x.com/cgtwts)<br/>·<br/>5h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow cgtwts<br/>6<br/>6<br/>24<br/>[3.8K](http://x.com/cgtwts/status/2065026197734797813/analytics)<br/>Most agency owners do not have a capacity problem. They have a repetition problem.<br/>The same proposal structure. The same onboarding questions. The same follow-up email when a client goes quiet. The same brief summary written three different ways for three different clients. Hours spent on work that keeps the business alive but does not move it forward.<br/>That is the problem. Here is what solving it actually looks like.<br/>The Business<br/>Marcus runs a one-person brand strategy consultancy. He works with three to five clients at a time, charges mid-to-high market rates, and had been stuck at the same revenue ceiling for two years. Not because he lacked clients. Because he kept running out of hours.<br/>His week looked like this: fifteen hours of billable work, twelve hours of admin, proposals, emails, and meeting prep. The math was broken before he even opened his laptop.<br/>Stage One: Audit Before You Automate<br/>The instinct is to jump straight into building prompts. That is the wrong move.<br/>Marcus spent one week logging every task he repeated. Not estimating. Logging. At the end of the week he had a list of twenty-three recurring tasks. Fourteen of them were language-based. Proposals, briefs, status updates, scope clarification emails, LinkedIn posts from workshop notes, onboarding documents.<br/>He did not need a new tool. He needed those fourteen tasks systematized.<br/>The prompt he used for this audit:<br/>&gt; "I run a solo brand strategy consultancy. Here is my typical week: \[pasted log\]. Identify which tasks are language-based and could be handled or accelerated with an AI writing system. Group them by frequency and time cost."<br/>Claude returned a prioritized list in under two minutes. The top three were proposals, client onboarding briefs, and project status summaries. Combined, they were eating nine hours a week.<br/>Stage Two: Build the System, Not the Shortcut<br/>This is where most business owners get it wrong.<br/>They ask Claude to write one proposal. It comes out decent. They feel good. Then next week they start from scratch again and wonder why results vary. That is not automation. That is assisted typing.<br/>Marcus built a Claude Project for each core workflow.<br/>For proposals, the project system prompt included his positioning, his pricing logic, his typical client objections, and three anonymized past proposals as style references. The project remembered context across every session. It did not need re-explaining. It did not forget his tone between uses.<br/>His proposal prompt:<br/>&gt; "Client: \[name and company\]. Industry: \[sector\]. Problem they described: \[paste discovery call notes\]. Budget signal: \[high/medium/unclear\]. Write a proposal using my standard structure. Keep the tone direct. Flag any section where you need more information before I send this."<br/>First draft in four minutes. Editing time down from ninety minutes to twenty. The output was not perfect. It never is. But it was eighty percent of the way there, and editing a draft is fundamentally faster than generating one from nothing.<br/>For onboarding, he built a second project. System prompt included his full onboarding process, the questions he asks every new client, his communication style, and a note about what tends to confuse new clients in the first two weeks. New client int</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 31 · 🔁 6 · 💬 9 · 🔖 39</p><p><a href="https://x.com/cgtwts/status/2065026197734797813">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
